hadiths[9]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17779","Al-Walid ibn Muslim Abu al-Abbas al-Dimashqi narrated to us at Makka from dictation who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to me who said: Yahya ibn Jabir al-Tai, the judge of Hims, narrated to me who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami narrated to me, from his father, that he heard al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Kilabi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the Dajjal one morning, and he lowered concerning him and raised, until we thought him among the cluster of palm trees. When we went to him in the evening he recognized that in our faces, so we asked him and said: O Messenger of Allah, you mentioned the Dajjal this morning, and you lowered concerning him and raised, until we thought him among the cluster of palm trees. He said: Other than the Dajjal is what frightens me for you. If he comes out while I am among you, I am his disputant instead of you. And if he comes out while I am not among you, each man is the disputant of himself, and Allah is my successor over every Muslim. He is a youth, curly-haired, intensely curly, his eye protruding. And he comes out from a passage between Sham and Iraq, and spreads corruption right and left. O servants of Allah, be firm. We said: O Messenger of Allah, how long is his stay in the earth. He said: Forty days, a day like a year, and a day like a month, and a day like a Friday, and the rest of his days like your days. We said: O Messenger of Allah, then that day which is like a year, will the prayer of a day and night suffice us in it. He said: No, estimate for it its measure. We said: O Messenger of Allah, how is his speed in the earth. He said: Like rain driven by the wind. He said: He passes by a tribe and calls them, and they respond to him. He orders the sky and it rains, and the earth and it grows, and their pasturing livestock return to them, with the longest humps they ever were, and the widest flanks, and the fullest udders. And he passes by a tribe and calls them, and they reject his word upon him, so their wealth follows him, and they wake up destitute with nothing of their wealth. And he passes by the ruin and says to it: Bring out your treasures, so its treasures follow him like swarms of bees. He said: And he orders a man and he is killed, and he strikes him with the sword and cuts him in two pieces like the throwing of a target, then he calls him and he comes to him with his face beaming. He said: While he is upon that, Allah, Almighty and Majestic, sends the Messiah son of Mary, peace be upon him, and he descends at the white minaret east of Damascus, between two dyed garments, placing his hand upon the wings of two angels. So he follows him and catches him and kills him at the eastern gate of Ludd. He said: While they are thus, Allah, Almighty and Majestic, reveals to Isa son of Mary, peace be upon him: I have brought out servants among My servants whom you have no power to fight, so shelter My servants to the Mount. So Allah, Almighty and Majestic, sends Yajuj and Majuj, and they are as Allah, Almighty and Majestic, said: From every elevation they hasten. So Isa and his companions beseech Allah, Almighty and Majestic, and He sends upon them worms in their necks, and they wake up dead together like the death of one soul. Then Isa and his companions descend, and they find no house in the earth but their stench and filth has filled it. So Isa and his companions beseech Allah, Almighty and Majestic, and He sends upon them birds like the necks of Bactrian camels, and they carry them and throw them where Allah, Almighty and Majestic, wills. Ibn Jabir said: Ata ibn Yazid al-Saksaki narrated to me, from Kab or another, who said: They throw them at al-Muhabbal. Ibn Jabir said: I said: O Abu Yazid, and where is al-Muhabbal. He said: The rising place of the sun. He said: And Allah, Almighty and Majestic, sends rain from which no house of hair nor clay is sheltered, for forty days, and it washes the earth until it leaves it like a mirror. And it is said to the earth: Grow your fruits and return your blessing. He said: On that day a group eats of a pomegranate and shades themselves with its peel, and blessing is put in milk, until a milch camel suffices a crowd of people, and a milch cow suffices a clan, and a sheep of goats suffices the people of a house. He said: While they are upon that, Allah, Almighty and Majestic, sends a good wind beneath their armpits, and it takes the soul of every Muslim, or he said: every believer, and the worst of people remain, coupling like the coupling of donkeys, and upon them, or he said: upon him, the Hour stands."
"17780","Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us who said: I heard, meaning Ibn Jabir, say: Busr ibn Ubaydullah al-Hadrami narrated to me that he heard Abu Idris al-Khawlani say: I heard al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Kilabi say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no heart but it is between two fingers among the fingers of the Lord of the worlds. If He wills to establish it, He establishes it, and if He wills to deviate it, He deviates it. And he used to say: O Turner of hearts, make our hearts firm upon Your religion. And the balance is in the hand of the Most Merciful, Almighty and Majestic, He lowers it and raises it."
"17781","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Muawiya, meaning Ibn Salih, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from his father, that al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Ansari said, and thus Zayd ibn al-Hubab said al-Ansari. He said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about righteousness and sin. He said: Righteousness is good character, and sin is what agitates in your chest and you hate that people should see it."
"17782","Abd al-Quddus Abu al-Mughira al-Khawlani narrated to us who said: Safwan, meaning Ibn Amr, narrated to us, Yahya ibn Jabir al-Tai narrated to us, from al-Nawwas ibn Saman who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about righteousness and sin. He said: Righteousness is good character, and sin is what agitates in your soul and you hate that people should know it."
"17783","Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami mention, from his father, from al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Ansari, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about righteousness and sin. He said: Righteousness is good character, and sin is what agitates in your soul and you hate that people should see it."
"17784","Al-Hasan ibn Sawwar Abu al-Ala narrated to us, Layth, meaning Ibn Sad, narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Salih, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr narrated to him, from his father, from al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah struck a parable of a straight path, and upon the two sides of the path are two walls, in them open doors, and upon the doors are lowered curtains. And upon the gate of the path is a caller saying: O people, enter the path all together and do not diverge. And a caller calls from inside the path. When one wants to open anything of those doors, he says: Woe to you, do not open it, for if you open it you will enter it. And the path is Islam, and the two walls are the limits of Allah Almighty, and the open doors are the prohibitions of Allah Almighty. And that caller upon the head of the path is the Book of Allah, Almighty and Majestic, and the caller above the path is the admonisher of Allah in the heart of every Muslim."
"17785","Umar ibn Harun narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Shurayh, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, from Nawwas ibn Saman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Great treachery, that you tell your brother speech in which he believes you while you lie about it."
"17786","Haywa ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Baqiyya narrated to us who said: Bahir ibn Sad narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-Nawwas ibn Saman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Almighty and Majestic, struck a parable of a straight path. Upon the two edges of the path are two walls, in them open doors, and upon the doors are curtains. And a caller calls upon the head of the path, and a caller calls from above it. And Allah calls to the abode of peace, and guides whom He wills to a straight path. The doors upon the two edges of the path are the limits of Allah. None falls into the limits of Allah until the veil of Allah is uncovered. And the one who calls from above it is the admonisher of Allah, Almighty and Majestic."
"17787","Yazid ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us who said: Al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muhajir, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr who said: I heard al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Kilabi say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Quran is brought on the Day of Resurrection and its people who used to act by it, preceded by Surat al-Baqara and Al Imran. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck three examples for them which I have not forgotten after. He said: As if they are two clouds, or two shades, or two black masses with a glow between them, as if they are two flocks of birds in rows, arguing for their companion."
